These do not establish the missing proofs, do not make scan operational, and
must not be bypassed by setting readiness flags optimistically. No change to
owner-controlled safe boot, USB/EFI installation or hardware testing.

## Source-only MCI coexistence query transmitter

`Ar9565Coexistence.cpp` now also has a bounded, owner-epoch MCI GPM
**version-query and Bluetooth status/topology-query transmitter** using the
exact pinned Linux ath9k `ar9003_mci_send_message` COMMAND0/TX_PAYLOAD
register sequence. It requires independently verified restored single-chain
board and coherent DMA/antenna/MCI-reset evidence, a same-epoch attached GPM
cursor, a genuine awake Bluetooth host/command lease and MAC auto-responses
quiesced. Its live MMIO preflight rechecks the rings, MCI enabled state,
masked interrupts and the hardware remote-sleep bit. A raw W1C command
completion/failed-send is explicitly acknowledged without erasing unrelated
GPM receive notifications. A 500-iteration poll with 1-us delays per retry
(plus MMIO overhead) and any ambiguous hardware
write/readback/owner loss retain the full-reset requirement. C++14 and C++17
ASan/UBSan fixtures cover both request encodings, stale W1C, timeout,
controller rejection, remote sleep, failures and revoked epochs; direct
Clang-Tidy passes. The native PCI or USB Bluetooth owner does **not invoke**
this primitive, and the GPM response remains unconsumed/unverified. It
does **not** establish Bluetooth negotiation, physical coexistence, active
RF, Wi-Fi, scanning or AirDrop readiness. The RF/PHY/regulatory TODO remains
open until native board/clock/regulatory proof and complete RF + BT leases
are actually implemented.

A follow-up fail-closed transaction audit additionally revokes the attached
MCI GPM cursor on a partial command write, unreadable or ambiguous RAW status,
controller-rejected query, 500-us timeout, or same-epoch owner replacement
inside a synchronous command callback. Even a caller that ignores the
`requiresReset` result can no longer resend with the stale cursor; only
the independently gated physical reset path can reattach it. Clean preflight
denials leave the cursor attached and perform no writes. Synthetic regression
coverage includes rejected second sends, stale callbacks and unreadable
status before transmission. These are owner-safety constraints, **not**
proof of a live Bluetooth host handshake or physical MCI bus arbitration.

The source-only GPM RX reader now also exposes a **read-only coex notice
diagnostic** for copied 16-byte MCI messages. Based on the pinned Linux
`ar9003_mci_process_gpm_extra`, `ath_mci_msg`, `ar9003_mci.h` and `mci.h`,
this recognizes inbound version/status queries, peer Bluetooth version
responses and profile/status notifications. It rejects detached/stale epochs,
message header/byte mismatches, invalid profile types and out-of-range BT
status fields, and parses the status sequence as explicit little-endian
bytes. The parser does not touch MMIO/DMA, change antenna priorities or
interpret a message as successful BT negotiation. Synthetic C++14/C++17
fixtures cover recognized, irrelevant and malformed notices. The native
owner still must synchronize physical GPM DMA, handle actual response
transactions and prove host Bluetooth arbitration before any real use.

The command sender now snapshots its owner-epoch GPM cursor index before
MMIO and checks it both before TX payload submission and before returning
SW_MSG_DONE success. Pre-send callback mutations cannot issue COMMAND0;
post-command cursor detachment or index changes revoke the cursor and demand
reset. Sanitized tests cover both exact same-epoch mutation intervals.
**This does not detect an ABA reattach that restores the same index**:
the eventual native owner must additionally enforce unique reset generation
and exclusive serialization across Bluetooth, GPM DMA, reset and commands.

## Source progress — 2026-11-10: bounded ath9k ANI timing-error adaptation

The production-target `Ar9565Ani.{hpp,cpp}` now has a separately callable
AR9003 timing-error monitor (`adaptAr9565AniToPhyErrors`) above the already
implemented physical INI first-step/spur register writer. Using *completed*,
independently verified same-epoch OFDM/CCK PHY-error windows and **PHY listen
time** (not wall time or EDMA drops), it implements ath9k's >300-ms
threshold/hysteresis: OFDM high/low thresholds depend on the current OFDM
level, CCK has its own thresholds, low-error decisions alternate, and the
most restrictive OFDM/CCK first-step is applied with ath9k's 0..8 OFDM
level-to-first-step/spur table. A hardware callback must authorize every
MMIO access; a failed program/readback invalidates the baseline and requires
reset. Each observed window is scoped to the current owner epoch and a strictly
increasing counter sequence, preventing the same PHY-error window from being
applied twice. Saturated 24-bit counters and unknown provenance fail closed.

The level-9 OFDM branch (which also changes weak-signal detection) and AR9565
unsupported CCK MRC are intentionally **not** activated; until a reliable
per-associated-BSSID RSSI lease exists, CCK immunity caps at the ath9k
low-RSSI-safe level 6. The synthetic `tests/test_ar9565_ani.cpp` now exercises
OFDM/CCK rise/fall, no-op tuning, full threshold boundaries, replayed/cross-
epoch counter rejection, 24-bit saturation, 64-bit rate scaling, bad MMIO,
owner revocation and stale channel state under C++14 and C++17 ASan+UBSan.
Focused strict Clang-Tidy and Clang-Format on changed units pass. This is an
offline source-only adapter, **not** a physical ANI poller or a radio-enable
path. The native PCI owner must still initialize PHY-error masks, read and
validate MIB cycle/PHY counters over bounded hardware listen-time windows,
provide independently authorized per-channel epochs, schedule the observations,
and wire the resulting policy into the real receiver after RF/country/BT
arbitration. The P1 source TODO and P0 full integration remain open.

## Follow-up: physical MIB counter window derivation (source only)

`deriveAr9565AniErrorWindow` now accepts two explicitly sourced, same-channel
AR_CCCNT / AR_RFCNT / AR_TFCNT and AR_PHY_ERR_1/2 snapshot values. It computes
PHY listen time from the original Linux ath9k 2.4-GHz OFDM MAC rates (44 MHz
HT20, 88 MHz HT40), subtracts RX/TX frame cycles, validates monotonically
increasing snapshots, 24-bit PHY counters, owner epoch, capture sequence,
channel/width, and a <=5-second monotonic elapsed-time upper bound. It rejects
counter regressions, saturation, impossible busy-cycle sums and absent
hardware/MMIO/mask evidence. The output feeds the new OFDM/CCK ANI adapter,
and C++14/C++17 sanitized fixtures cover HT20/HT40 conversion and invalid
windows. It is not a collector: a real native MIB owner must still own and
sample the counters, ensure no concurrent MIB clear/wrap or channel change,
configure timing-error masks, schedule measured windows, and run adaptation
under a valid receive-only radio policy. No change here enables RF.

### Physical ANI/MIB sampler source (November 10, 2026)

`src/hardware/Ar9565AniMib.{hpp,cpp}` adds a production-target, real-MMIO
counter acquisition adapter based on pinned ath9k `ani.c`, `ath/hw.c`,
`ath/reg.h` and `ath9k/reg.h`. Given a native owner's *exclusive* MIB lease,
verified same-epoch channel/INI evidence and monotonic timestamp, it freezes
AR_MIBC, zeros the cycle/PHY error counters, programs and checks OFDM/CCK
masks, then unfreezes. Later captures freeze CCCNT/RFCNT/TFCNT and
PHY_ERR_1/2 with mask/readback checks and a bounded five-second sampling
interval. It hands real counter snapshots to `deriveAr9565AniErrorWindow`.
No transmit, receiver authorization or synthetic counter data is introduced.

Every physical access rechecks the lease; partial/ambiguous writes, changed
masks, saturated counters, stale sampling or a lost epoch invalidate the
counter session. The collector separately rejects invalid HT20/HT40 channel
geometry and changed silicon/reference-crystal identity, and invalidates an
active counter epoch when physical RF authorization disappears. A same-epoch
restart cannot erase its reset obligation. C++14/C++17 offline sanitized PCI
fixtures cover paired captures, malformed geometry, missing radio permission,
read/write faults, callback revocation, MIB drift and reinitialization.

### Receive-only RF sequence initializes physical ANI counters

`Ar9565RadioSequence` now invokes `initializeAr9565AniMib` after the
successful receiver AGC/IQ/noise calibration and verified ANI INI-register
baseline. The caller supplies a real monotonic-millisecond starting time and
an independently authorized exclusive MIB lease. Preflight requires the MIB
read/write callbacks, BAR length, context and epoch to match the physical
radio backends, so a different register transport cannot silently supply a
synthetic counter baseline. The zero-time and mismatched-backend checks occur
before hardware writes. MIB initialization failures retain the existing
full-PHY-reset obligation, and a lost final owner invalidates both ANI and
MIB state instead of publishing a reusable stale counter session.

The existing RF workflow fixture now compiles the production MIB collector
and verifies zeroed counters, actual OFDM/CCK mask register writes, combined
PHY/MIB completion, wrong-transport/epoch rejection, zero timestamp, failed
mask readback, ambiguous MIB writes and final owner revocation. Its focused
C++14/C++17 ASan+UBSan runs, direct strict Clang-Tidy and Clang-Format pass.
No physical radio operations were performed.

**Still required:** `SimpleATH` must call the receive-only RF sequence only
after independent board/reference-crystal, country/channel, antenna and
Bluetooth coexistence evidence exists; it currently does not invoke it.
The native owner must claim exclusive MIB counter ownership across all users,
retain the resulting ANI/MIB epoch, schedule and attest paired counter
captures, then safely apply PHY-error adaptation. Physical radio activation,
macOS kernel MMIO and AirDrop operation remain unverified by host fixtures.
